				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} H(G)=\sum _{uv \in E}\left( \frac{2}{d_v+d_u}\right) . \end{aligned}$$\end{document}In this study, we are considering the NM-polynomial to compute the indices. The M–polynomial of Deutsch and Klavžar encodes edge–degree information of a graph in a bivariate polynomial and is widely used to generate degree-based indices29. Neighbourhood M–polynomial indices (or simply NM–polynomial indices) were introduced by Mondal et al. as a powerful extension of this idea30. Unlike the M–polynomial, which uses only the vertex degrees, the NM–polynomial incorporates the neighbourhood degree information, i.e. the sum of the degrees of the neighbours of each vertex. This richer encoding makes it possible to derive a large family of degree– and neighbourhood–based descriptors systematically.

